      "stageRecord": "Build the demonstrative DP Ces lettres, the proper-name DPs Léa and Paul, the pronominal DP les, and the verbal projection. Proper names occupy nominal projections with silent determiners; les is a saturated pronominal D. The participial V initially has no surface word because its agreement has not yet been established. The auxiliary a is introduced separately as finite I. Within V′, envoyer selects the direct-object DP as Theme and the à-PP as Goal. This analysis permits the two ordered complements within one V′; the PP is a verbal complement, not a modifier of lettres. The preposition à governs the DP Paul and licenses its oblique Case. The verb licenses accusative Case on its object DP. Finally, Ces agrees with lettres in feminine plural features. The clitic's underlying feminine plural features are compatible with the intended antecedent, although les itself does not overtly distinguish gender. The topic DP is still separate and has not yet acquired a resumptive dependency.",
